<% content_for :title, "Budget" %>

<%# THE ONE DOOR TO A HAND-MADE RULE (Henry's ruling of 2026-08-20). `/budgets/new` existed and
    nothing linked to it: every other way into that form carries an owner already (the Edit links
    below, the suggestion panel's accept links), so a user who wanted a rule for something their
    entries have not shown yet had no door at all. It belongs HERE and only here, because this is
    the screen that lists rules — the same reason the form's Cancel and both of its saves come back
    to this path.

    `standard_action_button` rather than markup of its own, which is what puts it in the same
    treatment as the New Category button on the categories index. %>
<%= page_header(title: "Budget",
                subtitle: "The rules that fill your categories",
                actions: [standard_action_button(label: "New rule", url: new_budget_path, icon: "plus")]) %>

<div class="space-y-6">
  <% if @presenter.no_rules? %>
    <%= render "empty" %>
  <% else %>
    <%# The fill order itself: category by category, top to bottom, exactly as a distribution
        spends. One list — the account bands are gone with the per-account waterfall. %>
    <% groups = @presenter.category_groups %>
    <%= render "fill_order", groups: groups if groups.any? %>

    <%# Rules exist but none of them names a category. TRANSITIONAL (Task 8 closes it):
        `Budget.for_user` still spans both owner lanes, so a rule written before the cutover names
        only a pool and no group on this page can show it. Said plainly rather than left as a gap
        above nothing, which would read as a section that failed to render. %>
    <% if groups.empty? %>
      <section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded px-6 py-4">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
          Nothing is in the fill order yet — none of your rules names a category that holds money.
        </p>
      </section>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# OUTSIDE the no_rules? branch, deliberately. §8 puts the check under the rules, but a
      brand-new user with no rules is exactly the person who has declared nothing — sending the
      block away with the empty state would leave the only writer for `typical_income`,
      `period_cadence` and `period_anchor_date` in the app unreachable until they had made a rule.
      The check reads $0.00 for them, which is true. %>
  <%= render "structural_check", presenter: @presenter %>

  <%# §8'S BOTTOM HALF, and it renders for EVERY user — including the one with no rules at all,
      whose top half is the empty state. That user is exactly who this panel is for: a brand-new
      account sees an empty top half and a full bottom one, which is the spec's own reason there
      is no separate onboarding flow to build or maintain. %>
  <%= render "suggestions", presenter: @presenter %>
</div>
